me', u'*a*'), Wildcard(u'bids:TaskName', u'*a*'), Wildcard(u'bids:age(years)', u'*a*'), Wildcard(u'bids:participant_id', u'*a*'), Wildcard(u'citation', u'*a*'), Wildcard(u'comment\u003cBIDSVersion\u003e', u'*a*'), Wildcard(u'comment\u003cparticipant#sex\u003e', u'*a*'), Wildcard(u'conformsto', u'*a*'), Wildcard(u'description', u'*a*'), Wildcard(u'license', u'*a*'), Wildcard(u'name', u'*a*'), Wildcard('parentds', u'*a*'), Wildcard('path', u'*a*'), Wildcard('type', u'*a*')])\n\n```","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1518255604,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM2NDYzOTI2Mw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2052#issuecomment-364639263"},"message":"I can no longer replicate this with #2127.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1518549151,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM2NTM3MjA0OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2052#issuecomment-365372048"},"message":"So here is what happens on the new big search index (1M records) and current #2157:\n\n```\n% datalad search '*ab*'\n[INFO   ] Query completed in 8.3466770649 sec. Reporting up to 20 top matches. \nsearch(ok): openfmri/ds000030/phenotype/spanish_vocab.tsv (file)\n...\ndatalad search '*ab*'  8.82s user 0.11s system 100% cpu 8.882 total\n```\n\nWhich is slooow.\n\nThe same with `'*a*` will take \"forever\". The reason is that whoosh does not simply compile a sequential list of hits, but scores them to provide the \"best\" hits first: it is a search engine, not grep. With `*a*` it is tasked to find the best combination of any occurences of a single character `a` in any of 1M candidate string. I do not see the point in such a query, but if it is needed to support that, we better implement a frontend for a `grep` through a metadata dump.\n\nI will close this now.","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1518549151,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MTQ3MjMzMTE5OQ=="},"status":2},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1518636415,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM2NTcxNzc4MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2052#issuecomment-365717780"},"message":"I kept thinking about this. We do not actually need to implement anything in other to support \"grep-through-all-metadata\". The following is done right after `datalad install ///` -- not index building or preprocessing whatsoever:\n\n```\n% datalad -f '{path}: {metadata}' metadata -r . --reporton datasets | grep -i Halchenko | cut -d ':' -f1\n/tmp/hotshit/dbic\n/tmp/hotshit/labs/gobbini\n/tmp/hotshit/labs/gobbini/famface/data\n/tmp/hotshit/labs/haxby\n/tmp/hotshit/labs/haxby/attention\n/tmp/hotshit/labs/haxby/life\n/tmp/hotshit/labs/haxby/raiders\n/tmp/hotshit/openfmri\n/tmp/hotshit/openfmri/ds000233\n/tmp/hotshit/openfmri/ds000241\n/tmp/hotshit/workshops/mind-2017\n/tmp/hotshit/workshops/nih-2017\n/tmp/hotshit/workshops/nipype-2017\n```\n\nOn the present state of `///` this has a sub-second runtime. The \"worst case\" grepping through every known bit of metadata (datasets and files) does not even take a minute for 1M files (with metadata) across 490 datasets:\n\n```\n% datalad -f '{path}: {metadata}' metadata -r . --reporton all |grep -i Halchenko | cut -d ':' -f1 \n/tmp/hotshit/dbic\n/tmp/hotshit/labs/gobbini\n/tmp/hotshit/labs/gobbini/famface/data\n/tmp/hotshit/labs/haxby\n/tmp/hotshit/labs/haxby/attention\n/tmp/hotshit/labs/haxby/life\n/tmp/hotshit/labs/haxby/raiders\n/tmp/hotshit/openfmri\n/tmp/hotshit/openfmri/ds000233\n/tmp/hotshit/openfmri/ds000241\n/tmp/hotshit/workshops/mind-2017\n/tmp/hotshit/workshops/nih-2017\n/tmp/hotshit/workshops/nipype-2017\ndatalad -f '{path}: {metadata}' metadata -r . --reporton all  45.68s user 1.53s system 100% cpu 47.004 total\n```\n\nGranted, our simplistic output formatter keeps the markup of the `metadata` records as JSON, but otherwise one's full shell script foo can be unleashed.\n\n@yarikoptic @bpoldrack","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1518637667,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM2NTcyMzc5MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2052#issuecomment-365723790"},"message":"Nice. I think that's sufficient. We can simply have a `datalad-simple-search` script, that does the above, passing its argument into grep and may be (optionally) do a little beautification of json output.\n\nEdit:\nPlus some doc, refering to that script as an example on how to do it.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1518640133,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM2NTczNTE0NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2052#issuecomment-365735144"},"message":"Or `search --mode regex`","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1518647255,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM2NTc2NTk0Mg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2052#issuecomment-365765942"},"message":"Congratulations! Just add ability to print only specific fields in metadata, and we have converged on the old style search 100% just implemented straight in python without shell ;-)","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1518675907,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM2NTgzNjY0NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2052#issuecomment-365836644"},"message":"Except that we have nested metadata (requires a parser to actually get the depth-structure). In the mood to write that?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1518677183,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM2NTgzOTQyOA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2052#issuecomment-365839428"},"message":"Hold on, I was again fooled into thinking we cannot do that. But no. `--output-format` can be used to report arbitrary fields (and combinations even any depth AFAICR) in any result for any command. Hence also for `search`. The issue was that the old search never was a new-style command, hence it had all custom options and procedures.","files":null}]}